  "notes": [
    "Admission to 262,144 hands/seed requires all integrity/mechanism gates, >=2/3 positive 16k-to-65k slopes, nonnegative median final seed-holdout and seed-seat deltas, all three source agreements >=95%, and every partition >=90%. The prior 32k optimizer-dose result is a fixed comparator, not a checkpoint-selection pool.",
    "Matched 256-step comparison against the fixed prior full-dose 32k endpoint gave half-minus-full pooled deltas +7.05, -52.53, and +1.76 bb/100; two of three are positive, but seed1 is materially adverse and the half-dose endpoint median pooled delta is -10.91 versus +41.63 for full-dose 32k."
  ],
  "result": {
    "summary": "The exact preregistered half-update replication completed three independent 65,536-hand seeds (196,608 actual environment hands), 55,296 untouched paired holdout trajectories, and 12,288 source-panel queries. All 768 MGDA updates had positive worst-group geometry and every integrity, breadth, and Standard10 preservation gate passed. Final seed-holdout and seed-seat medians were +0.98 and +4.40 bb/100, but 16k-to-65k slopes were +57.11, -18.34, and -12.59, so only one of three seeds was positive. All 249 tests passed.",
    "conclusion": "Halving updates repaired the source-preservation failure but did not make the 65k learning direction reproducible: the preregistered two-of-three positive-slope gate failed. This is specific dose-response evidence against scaling the current half-update broad-MGDA recipe, not evidence that 65k hands should solve Slumbot or that long-horizon learned-policy training in general is futile.",
    "decision": "HOLD_HALF_UPDATE_BROAD_MGDA_SCALE",
    "next_step": "Run a compact read-only matched-dose evidence audit to quantify paired half65k versus full32k behavior and identify whether seed heterogeneity is attributable to opponent/seat partitions. Then choose a materially different scalable learned-weight training intervention rather than increasing this failed half-update recipe."
  },
